/* Pass 19B: canon-exact Danse and Snape chapter revisions. */
(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-50":{"number":50,"title":"A Name Above the Door","content":"The old sign came down in two pieces.\n\nOne half still said RESTRICTED ACCESS. The other had been painted over so many times that the original letters showed through in the rain.\n\nDanse stood beneath the empty brackets with a screwdriver in his hand.\n\n\"I should have changed it months ago,\" he said.\n\n\"You changed the door first,\" I answered.\n\nHe looked toward the entrance.\n\nThe workshop was open. Not unguarded, not careless, but designed for the people who used it. Edda could reach the bell. Ash could close the private workroom from inside. Renn and Aster had signed the repair schedule with their actual hours. The emergency exit opened without waiting for a former soldier to approve it.\n\nThe sign was the last thing that still spoke in the old voice.\n\nTamsin arrived carrying a fresh board.\n\n\"What are we calling it?\" she asked.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"It's your workshop.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\nIt had taken him a long time to understand that choosing a name did not require someone else to grant him a title.\n\nHe looked at the board.\n\n\"Brasswatch Open Workshop,\" he said.\n\nTamsin smiled.\n\n\"Good. I can fit that.\"\n\nShe painted the letters in a deep blue that matched the safe-route signs. Beneath them she added smaller words, approved by the people who worked there:\n\nREPAIRS • TRAINING • SHELTER BY CHOICE.\n\nDanse read the line.\n\n\"Should it say no rank required?\"\n\nRenn, standing nearby, laughed.\n\n\"Only if you promise not to invent a new one.\"\n\nDanse smiled.\n\n\"No ranks.\"\n\nWe raised the sign together.\n\nIt wasn't a ceremony. Tamsin held the ladder, Renn guided the brackets, and I passed up the screws. Danse did the final tightening, then climbed down and stepped back.\n\nThe board was level.\n\nThe door was open.\n\nFor a moment none of us spoke.\n\nThen Ash came out of the private room and looked at the sign.\n\n\"Better,\" they said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nAsh did not hug him, forgive him, or tell him he'd become a different person. They went back inside to finish their work.\n\nHe let them.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nThe opening day drew a few people who had avoided the workshop before. One brought a broken kettle. Another asked whether there was a safe place to repair a wheeled cart. A young traveler wanted to learn how to read a pressure gauge.\n\nRenn ran the beginner's lesson. Danse watched for a while, then stepped away when it became clear they didn't need him hovering.\n\nI caught his expression.\n\n\"Hard?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"You're doing well.\"\n\n\"An alarming statement.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe young traveler's first lesson did not go as planned.\n\nRenn had set out a pressure gauge, two small hoses and a tray for collecting water. The student, Ilya's cousin Niko, followed every instruction carefully until the second fitting refused to catch. Their hands began to shake.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Niko said.\n\n\"What for?\" Renn asked.\n\n\"I've wasted the lesson.\"\n\nDanse stood a few steps away, very still.\n\nI could see the sentence he'd been trained to say years ago: *You were given clear instructions.* He had no intention of using it, but the struggle to find different words showed in his face.\n\nRenn took the gauge from Niko.\n\n\"The fitting is worn,\" they said. \"I should have checked it before the lesson.\"\n\nNiko looked surprised.\n\n\"It isn't my fault?\"\n\n\"It might have been a mistake even if it were,\" Renn said. \"That would be part of learning.\"\n\nDanse stepped closer.\n\n\"Would you like to see how we test whether a fitting is safe to reuse?\"\n\nNiko nodded.\n\n\"All right. We will start there.\"\n\nHe didn't take the tools and do the repair himself. He showed the student where the thread had flattened, then handed the piece back and waited while Niko compared it with the new fitting.\n\nThe student found the difference.\n\n\"That's why it won't catch.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Danse said.\n\nThe word held no threat.\n\nI looked toward the open exit, where the safe-route sign had been set at a height Edda could read. People were working at their own benches, answering questions, arguing about measurements, earning their wages. A workshop rather than a barracks.\n\nLater Niko asked whether a lesson could be scheduled for next week.\n\nRenn checked the calendar.\n\n\"We can. I'll have a better fitting ready.\"\n\nDanse smiled.\n\nI found him afterward washing the used tools.\n\n\"You looked frightened when Niko apologized,\" I said.\n\n\"I was remembering what happened to people who made mistakes under my command.\"\n\nHe shut off the water.\n\n\"I can't change those moments.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I can make sure this room doesn't repeat them.\"\n\nI put the dry cloth beside him.\n\n\"That sounds like a good reason to keep the door open.\"\n\nHe looked at me with a small, tired tenderness.\n\n\"One of several,\" he said.\n\nAt noon, Toph arrived to inspect the new support beam. She tapped the stone, listened for the familiar movement, and declared the repair satisfactory under the conditions of the test.\n\nDanse checked the written measurement.\n\nIt matched.\n\n\"Almost competent,\" Toph said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nShe grinned and left before he could thank her.\n\nThe afternoon moved on. A lantern flickered at the edge of the yard, and someone asked Pip to replace the wick. Mara delivered bread for the volunteers. The generator ran within its safe load.\n\nNothing dramatic happened.\n\nI thought Danse might be disappointed.\n\nInstead he looked almost peaceful.\n\nThe last repair of the day belonged to a young traveler named Niko, who returned carrying the worn fitting from that morning and a question about the inspection tag.\n\n“It says rejected,” they told Danse. “Does that mean I failed?”\n\n“No,” he said. “It means the fitting failed inspection.”\n\nNiko considered the distinction, then turned the metal piece over in their fingers. “Could we keep it for the training shelf?”\n\nDanse's first instinct was visible. The soldier in him wanted every defective part removed from circulation and accounted for. The teacher had to decide whether a lesson could be kept without becoming a hazard.\n\n“We can,” he said, “if it's marked, stored away from reusable stock, and reviewed by Renn.”\n\nRenn came over, read the label and wrote a second one in larger letters. *Demonstration only — unsuitable for installation.* They put the piece in a clear container and asked Niko to explain to the next student what was wrong with it. Danse listened rather than answering for them.\n\nLater, a trader asked whether the workshop would repair his generator in exchange for a month's use of the old storage room. Ash looked up from their bench.\n\n“We don't bargain with other people's space,” they said.\n\nDanse felt the old pull of a convenient solution. I could see it in his jaw.\n\n“That's correct,” he said. “We'll give you a written repair quote. You'll need to arrange storage somewhere else.”\n\nThe trader grumbled and left without signing. Ash went back to work. Nobody made them thank Danse for respecting the door they'd already been promised.\n\nAt closing, Renn counted the tools, Edda checked the ramp and emergency latch, and Danse handed the cash ledger to the shift lead. For once the report did not finish with his signature alone.\n\nI found him staring at the row of names.\n\n“Something wrong?”\n\n“No,” he said. “I was looking for a place to put my rank. It appears I no longer need one.”\n\n“Sounds inefficient.”\n\nHe gave me a look. “Don't start.”\n\n“I'm very proud of you, Paladin Clipboard.”\n\nHe laughed, startled and genuine. Then he took off his gloves and put them away.\n\n\nAfter closing, we sat on the back steps with two cups of tea.\n\nHe stared at the new sign.\n\n\"I spent years believing I knew who I was because I wore armor,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know what I want to do when I take it off.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Repair things?\"\n\n\"Yes. Teach what I know. Learn what I don't. Make room for people who need shelter without demanding their names as payment.\"\n\nI felt warmth move through my chest.\n\n\"That sounds like a life.\"\n\nHe looked toward me.\n\n\"Would you like to be part of it?\"\n\n\"I already am.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"And you have other lives, other people you love. I know we agreed to that. I know you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want to ask you to abandon any of it.\"\n\n\"Then what are you asking?\"\n\n\"For the chance to keep choosing this with you. The ordinary days. The difficult talks. The mistakes we correct.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost relieved enough to laugh.\n\n\"That's a lot of responsibility.\"\n\n\"Good thing you're capable of writing a checklist.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him, slow and warm. His hand rested against the back of my neck, gentle and sure. We stayed close until the light above the workshop door came on and the yard grew quiet.\n\nLater, inside, the rest of the evening belonged to us. No rank, no audience, no need to make the tenderness visible to anyone else.\n\nIn the morning, Danse opened the workshop at the posted hour.\n\nRenn was already there with a student who wanted to learn how to replace a worn hinge. The kettle from yesterday had been repaired and was ready to collect. The emergency exit was clear.\n\nDanse put on his work gloves.\n\n\"Morning,\" he said.\n\n\"Morning,\" Renn answered.\n\nThey handed him the inspection sheet.\n\nHe read it, made one correction, and gave it back.\n\nThen he stepped aside so the student could begin.\n\nOutside, the sign caught the early light.\n\nBrasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nA name he'd chosen.\n\nA door anyone could ask to enter.\n\nA place he could leave at the end of the day and still choose to return.","word_count":1642}},"after-always":{"after-always-16":{"number":16,"title":"For Enemies","content":"The Ministry wanted a complete list of every spell Snape had invented.\n\nSnape wanted the Ministry to go to hell.\n\nI considered both positions understandable.\n\nThe request arrived by owl on a Tuesday morning in a cream envelope stamped with the Department of Magical Law Enforcement seal. Snape read it once, folded it into quarters, and placed it beneath a jar of flobberworm mucus.\n\n“That is not technically a response,” I said.\n\n“It has been filed.”\n\n“Under biological waste?”\n\n“Appropriately.”\n\nI moved the jar before the letter absorbed anything unpleasant.\n\n“The hearing is in two weeks. Ignoring them seems strategically unwise.”\n\n“So does providing the Ministry with a convenient catalogue of my private research.”\n\n“They already know about Sectumsempra, Levicorpus, Liberacorpus, Langlock, and Muffliato.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You have been studying.”\n\n“I have access to newspapers.”\n\n“A regrettable condition.”\n\n“And Harry's testimony is public.”\n\n“An even more regrettable condition.”\n\nI opened the letter.\n\n“They're asking for spells with possible evidentiary relevance.”\n\n“They are asking for everything and hoping I am too tired to object.”\n\nI reread the language.\n\nHe was right.\n\nThat annoyed me.\n\n“Fine,” I said. “We answer narrowly.”\n\n“We answer nothing.”\n\n“Do you want them assuming every undocumented spell is Dark magic?”\n\n“They do already.”\n\n“Do you want that assumption in the hearing record?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\nWe had been preparing for the hearing in pieces. Evidence boxes. Statements. Potion analyses. Letters from former students, some condemning him and some describing things he had done during the last year to protect them. Harry Potter had agreed to testify. McGonagall had agreed to testify. Several members of the Order had opinions so complicated they required appendices.\n\nSnape treated the process like a second war.\n\nEvery request was a possible trap.\n\nEvery friendly letter contained an angle.\n\nEvery compliment made him suspicious.\n\nI understood why.\n\nI was also tired of it.\n\n“Let's make our own categories,” I said.\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\n“Harmless utility. Defensive. Offensive but nonlethal. Dangerous. Dark.”\n\n“Childish.”\n\n“Functional.”\n\n“Those categories overlap.”\n\n“Then add notes.”\n\n“You are attempting to reduce spellcraft to a filing cabinet.”\n\n“You love filing cabinets when they're in your house.”\n\nHis eyes flashed.\n\nI smiled.\n\nThat argument still amused me.\n\nAt last he sat.\n\n“Fine.”\n\nI took out a fresh sheet.\n\n“Muffliato.”\n\n“Utility.”\n\n“Privacy.”\n\n“Utility.”\n\n“Potentially useful for espionage.”\n\n“By that standard, doors are Dark objects.”\n\n“Fair. Utility.”\n\nI wrote.\n\n“Langlock.”\n\n“Nonlethal offensive.”\n\n“Also useful during staff meetings?”\n\nHe stared.\n\nI wrote nonlethal offensive.\n\n“Levicorpus.”\n\nHis face changed.\n\n“Offensive.”\n\n“Nonlethal?”\n\n“Usually.”\n\n“That usually is doing a lot of work.”\n\n“Any jinx can become dangerous in the presence of stairs, cliffs, water, sharp objects, unstable ceilings, or idiots.”\n\n“So Hogwarts.”\n\n“Precisely.”\n\nI wrote the note.\n\n“Sectumsempra.”\n\n“Dangerous. Dark-adjacent, depending on definition.”\n\n“Not simply Dark?”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\n“You want honesty or a slogan?”\n\n“Honesty.”\n\n“Dark magic is not a tidy legal species. Some spells are classified by intent, some by mechanism, some by tradition, some because the Ministry wishes to appear decisive. Sectumsempra causes severe physical injury through magical laceration. It was designed for violence. Call it Dark if the form pleases you.”\n\n“What do you call it?”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Mine.”\n\nThe word was not proud.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nI wrote: dangerous offensive curse; created for use against enemies; capable of fatal blood loss; creator possesses counter-curse.\n\nSnape read over my shoulder.\n\n“Add that the counter-curse requires immediate application.”\n\nI did.\n\n“And that dittany alone is inadequate for severe wounds.”\n\nI did.\n\nHe sat back.\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“Would you teach it now?”\n\nThe question changed the room.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Even to an Auror?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“A healer, so they could recognize it?”\n\nHe paused.\n\n“The counter-curse, perhaps. Not the curse.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because knowledge is not morally neutral simply because it is knowledge.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That sounds like something you learned the hard way.”\n\nHis expression was dry. “Most durable lessons are.”\n\nWe worked down the list.\n\nA charm that prevented ink from being read over one's shoulder.\n\nA variation on a slicing charm that cut plant stems without bruising them.\n\nA spell for keeping cauldron fumes from clinging to fabric.\n\nI laughed at that one.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You invented a laundry charm.”\n\n“It is a laboratory charm.”\n\n“It keeps robes from smelling like boiled spleen.”\n\n“It prevents contamination.”\n\n“Laundry.”\n\n“I regret telling you.”\n\n“You're a domestic genius.”\n\n“Stop.”\n\nThere was a charm that warmed boots from the inside.\n\nI laughed harder.\n\nSnape threatened to terminate the interview.\n\n“Were you cold?” I asked.\n\nHis expression changed.\n\nThe humor left me at once.\n\nSpinner's End.\n\nToo-large clothes. Poor heating. A boy who knew magic existed but lived in a house where comfort was scarce.\n\n“Sorry,” I said.\n\nHe looked down at the list.\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\nOne word.\n\nNo defensiveness.\n\nI did not make it tragic.\n\nI wrote utility warming charm and moved on.\n\nBy afternoon, the list had become something stranger than evidence.\n\nIt was a portrait of a mind.\n\nPrivacy spells.\n\nSilencing spells.\n\nDefensive counters.\n\nCruel jokes.\n\nEfficient tools.\n\nA brutal curse.\n\nA warm-boot charm.\n\nNothing about Snape was ever only one thing.\n\nThe final spell on his list had no name.\n\n“What does it do?”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\nThen he lifted his wand and pointed at a cracked glass vial on the table.\n\nThe fracture sealed.\n\nI examined it.\n\nThe repair was perfect.\n\n“Reparo?”\n\n“No. Reparo restores form. This restores certain magical tolerances in potion glass after curse exposure.”\n\n“You invented it during the war?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For the school?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why no name?”\n\n“I had other concerns.”\n\nI smiled faintly.\n\n“What would you call it now?”\n\n“I would not.”\n\n“Everything deserves a name eventually.”\n\n“An unsubstantiated claim.”\n\nI tapped the parchment.\n\n“We need something for the hearing.”\n\n“Restitutio Vitri.”\n\n“Glass restoration.”\n\n“Accurate.”\n\n“Boring.”\n\n“Accuracy often is.”\n\nI looked at the repaired vial.\n\n“Not always.”\n\nHe caught the tone.\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\nI felt that now-familiar shift under my ribs.\n\nThe one I kept refusing to name.\n\nSnape looked away.\n\n“Submit the list,” he said.\n\n“With restrictions.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe noticed.\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“You said it.”\n\n“It is a word.”\n\n“It is your word.”\n\n“I do not own vocabulary.”\n\n“Half the castle would disagree.”\n\nHe reached for the flobberworm jar.\n\nI moved the list out of range.\n\nFor enemies, I thought.\n\nOnce, he had written those words beside the cruelest thing he created.\n\nNow he was choosing which knowledge to preserve, which to share, which to keep contained.\n\nIt was not redemption by paperwork.\n\nNothing was that simple.\n\nBut it was a choice.\n\nAnd then another.\n\nWe spent the next hour sorting the spells into categories.\n\nUseful. Reckless. Defensive. Cruel. Dependent on context. Never teach without supervision. Never teach at all.\n\nSnape objected to my labels until I made him propose better ones.\n\nHe did.\n\nOf course he did.\n\nMuffliato went under controlled utility with a note about consent and privacy. Levicorpus earned a long argument because its original use had become tangled with schoolyard humiliation from more than one direction. Sectumsempra remained alone on the final page.\n\n“What about a countercurse?” I asked.\n\nHis face sharpened.\n\n“I know one.”\n\n“Should other healers?”\n\nHe stared at the word for enemies on the copied page.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe answer cost him something.\n\nThe old page contained one additional sentence in the margin. It was in Severus's handwriting, younger and more arrogant than the annotations I knew.\n\n*For demonstration only.*\n\nHe stared at it until the candle guttered.\n\n“Did you ever demonstrate it?” I asked.\n\n“Once.” His voice had gone flat. “Not to a patient.”\n\nI wanted the familiar temptation of an easy answer. That he'd been a boy. That he'd been frightened. That he'd spent years paying for what he had become. But the ink in front of us wasn't asking whether he was capable of regret. It was asking what a healer would need if another injured person arrived.\n\nI took a clean page. “A medical warning should be usable by somebody who has never met you.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Then we cannot build it around your shame. Or your reputation.”\n\nHis expression flickered. “You have a gift for being insufferably accurate.”\n\n“We share a laboratory.”\n\nWe made two columns. One held the observable injury pattern and the known risks of delayed treatment. The other held uncertainties. He crossed out a flourish that made the remedy sound certain in every case.\n\n“No,” he said. “I have insufficient evidence for that.”\n\nI watched him write *requires specialist evaluation* in the margin. His hand didn't shake; he was choosing precision over pride.\n\nWhen we finished, I asked whether he wanted the note sent under his name.\n\n“Anonymous publication invites speculation about authenticity,” he said. “Use my name. Do not allow it to imply absolution.”\n\nI didn't say that made me proud. I slid the draft across and asked him to check the dosage notation one more time. He did, and corrected one of my figures with the almost vicious satisfaction of a man who would rather be useful than forgiven for being clever.\n\n\nWe drafted a sealed medical note describing the wound pattern and emergency response without including instructions for casting the curse itself.\n\n“You realize,” I said, “this may save someone from a spell you invented.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“That's not nothing.”\n\n“No.” His quill paused. “Nor is it enough to make inventing it admirable.”\n\n“I didn't say it was.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\nFor once, we did not need to argue the difference.\n\nAnd another.","word_count":1581},"after-always-17":{"number":17,"title":"The Language of Hands","content":"Snape's right hand improved before his left.\n\nI knew because he began stirring clockwise again without switching grip halfway through.\n\nHe knew I knew because he caught me watching.\n\n“Your fascination with my extremities is becoming conspicuous.”\n\nI nearly dropped the measuring spoon.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nThen, unbelievably, color rose along the tops of his cheekbones.\n\nI started laughing.\n\n“Do not.”\n\n“You said it.”\n\n“I did not mean—”\n\n“That makes it better.”\n\nHis expression became murderous.\n\nI leaned against the table, helpless.\n\n“Are you twelve?” he asked.\n\n“Currently, yes.”\n\n“You are impossible.”\n\n“So I've been told.”\n\nHis own line.\n\nThat only made me laugh harder.\n\nHe turned back to the cauldron with the stiff dignity of a man betrayed by language.\n\nWe were brewing a complex nerve restorative for St Mungo's, not for him this time. The hospital had heard about our audit work and sent a request for help reproducing a pre-war formulation whose maker had died. Snape knew the recipe. I knew the modern stabilizers. Between us, we had spent three days arguing it into something better.\n\nThe potion required paired work during the final stage.\n\nOne person had to stir continuously.\n\nThe other had to add twelve ingredients at exact intervals, some no more than four seconds apart.\n\nSnape took the stirring rod.\n\nI prepared the tray.\n\n“Ready?” I asked.\n\n“I was ready six minutes ago.”\n\n“You were complaining six minutes ago.”\n\n“I can do both.”\n\n“I've noticed.”\n\nWe began.\n\nPorcupine quill.\n\nThree turns.\n\nSilverleaf.\n\nSeven turns.\n\nA pinch of crushed moonstone.\n\nSnape's hand moved with elegant precision.\n\nI watched the potion color change from amber to pearl.\n\n“Now,” he said.\n\nI added salamander blood.\n\nThe surface flashed green.\n\n“Too fast.”\n\n“It was on time.”\n\n“Half a second early.”\n\n“You cannot possibly know that.”\n\n“I can count.”\n\n“So can I.”\n\n“Debatable.”\n\n“Keep stirring.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThe next addition required me to reach across him.\n\nI warned him first.\n\n“Left side.”\n\nHe shifted.\n\nMy forearm brushed his wrist.\n\nNo one froze.\n\nThat was new.\n\nWe continued.\n\nAt ingredient nine, his left hand began to tremble where it steadied the cauldron handle.\n\nHe ignored it.\n\nAt ingredient ten, the tremor worsened.\n\nI saw his jaw tighten.\n\n“Switch,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Your hand.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“Then switch.”\n\n“We lose the rhythm.”\n\n“I can take it.”\n\n“You have not practiced this sequence.”\n\n“I watched you.”\n\n“That is not equivalent.”\n\nThe potion darkened at the edge.\n\nWe had seconds.\n\nI reached for the stirring rod.\n\nSnape's hand closed over mine.\n\n“Wait.”\n\nHis grip was firm despite the tremor.\n\n“On my count,” he said.\n\nI understood.\n\nWe could transfer without breaking rhythm if our hands overlapped for a full turn.\n\n“One.”\n\nHis fingers tightened around mine on the rod.\n\n“Two.”\n\nWe stirred together.\n\n“Three.”\n\nHe released.\n\nI took over.\n\n“Counterclockwise now,” he said.\n\nI obeyed.\n\nHe added ingredient eleven with his right hand.\n\nThen twelve.\n\nThe potion flashed white.\n\nPerfect.\n\nWe both exhaled.\n\nMy hand remained on the stirring rod.\n\nHis hovered close.\n\nThe moment should have ended there.\n\nInstead he said, “Your grip was acceptable.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“Acceptable?”\n\n“Do not become emotional.”\n\n“I'm overwhelmed.”\n\n“Control yourself.”\n\n“I may need a chair.”\n\nHis mouth curved.\n\nThe teasing felt different after our hands had moved together.\n\nLess like defense.\n\nMore like play.\n\nThat frightened me more than sarcasm ever had.\n\nThe fourth ingredient was late by two seconds.\n\n“Stop,” Severus said.\n\nI froze with the little glass measure in my hand. The potion's surface had gone from blue to a cloudy green at the edges. My first instinct was to add more stabilizer, to prove I could rescue the work before he lost patience.\n\nHe caught the movement. “If you compensate without measuring, we shall have an expensive bottle of speculation.”\n\nI put the measure down.\n\nHe tested a sample in a clear dish, then compared it with the reference card we'd written that morning. The result wasn't dangerous, but it wasn't the formulation St Mungo's had requested. We could keep the batch for practice. We couldn't send it for a patient.\n\n“That's three hours,” I said.\n\n“Four, if you count my increasingly regrettable company.”\n\nI looked at his left hand. His grip on the rod was tight enough to whiten his knuckles, though he hadn't complained once.\n\n“You didn't tell me it was hurting.”\n\n“It was not relevant until it became relevant.”\n\n“That's a lovely definition. Completely useless.”\n\nHe glared at me. Then he released the rod and rolled his fingers open. There were faint red marks where the handle had pressed against his skin.\n\nWe took a break that cost us the batch but saved the rest of the afternoon. I fitted a thicker grip to the stirring rod, and he tested it without ceremony. He would not have thanked me for pity. He did, however, tell me exactly where the grip needed reshaping.\n\nWhen we began again, we assigned the timing aloud. He called the intervals; I counted the additions; neither pretended a mistake belonged to only one pair of hands. By the final stage, the surface stayed clear.\n\n“Acceptable,” Severus said.\n\n“From you, that's practically a serenade.”\n\n“Then your musical standards are as appalling as your arithmetic.”\n\nHe checked the vial seal himself, but he let me sign the production record beside him.\n\n\nWe bottled the potion.\n\nLater, while cleaning, I cut my thumb on a chipped vial.\n\nIt was shallow.\n\nI hissed.\n\nSnape turned immediately.\n\n“Show me.”\n\n“It's nothing.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Right. I hear it now.”\n\n“Your hypocrisy is refreshing.”\n\nI held out my hand.\n\nSnape took it.\n\nNot my wrist.\n\nMy hand.\n\nHis fingers supported my palm while he examined the cut.\n\nThe contact was practical.\n\nIt still changed my breathing.\n\nHe noticed.\n\nOf course he did.\n\nHis eyes lifted briefly to mine, then back to the injury.\n\n“Stay still.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“You moved.”\n\n“I breathed.”\n\n“Unnecessarily dramatic.”\n\nHe cleaned the cut with his wand and applied a drop of dittany.\n\nThe sting made me twitch.\n\nHis thumb pressed lightly against the base of mine to steady me.\n\nWarm.\n\nCareful.\n\nHis hands were not soft. There were calluses along his fingers from years of knives, quills, cauldrons, wands.\n\nI had noticed them before.\n\nNot like this.\n\nThe cut sealed.\n\nHe did not let go immediately.\n\nNeither did I pull away.\n\nHis gaze returned to my face.\n\nThe room narrowed.\n\nI thought he might say something sharp to break it.\n\nHe did not.\n\nInstead his thumb moved once across my palm.\n\nBarely.\n\nAn accident, perhaps.\n\nExcept Severus Snape did very little by accident when he was watching himself.\n\nMy pulse kicked.\n\nHe released me.\n\n“There.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nHe turned to the sink.\n\nI stared at my healed thumb.\n\nRidiculous.\n\nI was an adult. A touch was a touch. A healer might have done the same. A colleague might have.\n\nBut Snape had rules about contact.\n\nSo did I.\n\nWe had spent weeks teaching each other that touch required intention or necessity.\n\nThe cut had been necessary.\n\nThe extra second had not.\n\nNeither had the thumb across my palm.\n\nI looked at his back.\n\n“Snape.”\n\nHis shoulders tensed.\n\n“Yes?”\n\nI could ask.\n\nI could name it.\n\nI could force the moment into language and watch him retreat behind every wall he owned.\n\nSo I chose something else.\n\n“Your left hand did well today.”\n\nHe was quiet.\n\n“Better,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe turned.\n\nThere was gratitude in his face and something warmer beneath it.\n\n“Yours,” he said, “was adequate.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Careful. Compliments twice in one day.”\n\n“A temporary lapse.”\n\n“I'll document it.”\n\n“I will destroy the record.”\n\n“Then I'll make copies.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed with familiar amusement.\n\nWe cleaned the rest of the laboratory side by side.\n\nAt the end of the night, he handed me my coat.\n\nOur fingers touched.\n\nThe change was most obvious when I stopped looking for it.\n\nA month earlier, every accidental touch had become an event. A brush of fingers over a jar. His hand at my elbow on the stairs. Mine hovering near his back when his balance failed but refusing to land without permission.\n\nNow touch had begun to acquire grammar.\n\nA palm held out meant help me up.\n\nTwo fingers against my wrist meant stop before you ruin that measurement.\n\nThe back of his knuckles against my sleeve meant move; I am carrying something dangerous and apparently words are beneath me.\n\nOne evening I found him struggling with a stopper that had sealed itself too tightly. His left hand was trembling.\n\n“Want me?” I asked.\n\nHe froze.\n\nI realized how the question sounded at exactly the same moment he did.\n\nHeat climbed my face.\n\n“The jar,” I said.\n\n“I understood.”\n\n“You absolutely did not.”\n\nHis mouth curved with slow cruelty. “Your phrasing was imprecise.”\n\nI snatched the jar from him, opened it, and shoved it back.\n\n“Fixed.”\n\n“Remarkably efficient.”\n\n“Say one more thing.”\n\nHe looked directly at me. “Want me?”\n\nI nearly dropped the stopper.\n\nHis laugh rasped out before he could stop it.\n\nThat night, the language of hands gained sarcasm too.\n\nThis time neither of us pretended it was accidental.","word_count":1533},"after-always-28":{"number":28,"title":"After","content":"The morning after our first kiss, Severus Snape became aggressively professional.\n\nHe called me “Consultant” three times before breakfast.\n\nThe first was irritating.\n\nThe second was funny.\n\nBy the third, I understood he was terrified.\n\n“Consultant,” he said, holding out the Ministry file, “you will need this.”\n\nI took it.\n\n“Thank you, Professor.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“Not currently.”\n\n“Neither am I a consultant until nine.”\n\n“It is eight forty-seven.”\n\n“Then stop flirting with my job title.”\n\nHis mouth opened.\n\nClosed.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Cruel,” he said.\n\n“You survived.”\n\nHe looked away before I could see whether he smiled.\n\nWe were leaving for the Ministry at nine thirty.\n\nThe castle seemed offensively normal. Students ate porridge. Owls arrived. Someone dropped a goblet. No trumpet announced that Severus Snape had kissed me in a storeroom between nettles and unicorn horn.\n\nI was grateful.\n\nI was also disappointed, which was ridiculous.\n\nSnape ate almost nothing.\n\nI pushed toast toward him.\n\nHe pushed it back.\n\n“Not today.”\n\n“You need food.”\n\n“I need not to vomit during testimony.”\n\n“That is a fair point.”\n\nHe looked at me as if expecting argument.\n\n“See?” I said. “I can be reasonable.”\n\n“Disturbing.”\n\nBeneath the table, his knee touched mine.\n\nNeither of us moved it.\n\nThat tiny contact steadied us more than conversation.\n\nMcGonagall caught us at the end of breakfast with a small envelope and the expression she reserved for students who were old enough to know better.\n\n“The Ministry has changed the witness entrance,” she told Severus. “Reporters have been gathering at the public lifts.”\n\nHe reached for the envelope. She kept hold of it until he looked up.\n\n“Severus. I am telling you because you deserve the choice, not because I am instructing you to hide.”\n\nHis face hardened. “A distinction with which I am not entirely unfamiliar.”\n\n“I know,” she said. “And I should have made it more often.”\n\nI pretended to study the breakfast menu while both of them stood with years of unfinished history between their hands. The porridge had gone cold. Somewhere down the table a student laughed at a joke that had nothing to do with war.\n\nWhen she left, I asked whether he wanted me beside him at the public entrance.\n\n“You need not place yourself before cameras on my account.”\n\n“That's not what I asked.”\n\n“No,” he said, looking annoyed with himself. “It is not.”\n\nI had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory; I had never offered to make him a secret, and I had never asked him to be a public exhibit either.\n\n“If we walk in together, people may invent things,” I said. “If we walk separately, they'll invent different things. Which choice do you actually want?”\n\nHe put the envelope on the table and considered it as though it contained a difficult examination problem.\n\n“The witness entrance,” he said at last. “Because I want testimony heard before gossip. Not because I am ashamed of your company.”\n\n“And after?”\n\nHis gaze met mine. “After, you may have the satisfaction of being seen leaving with an exceptionally disagreeable man.”\n\n“It's a date, then.”\n\n“It is a logistical arrangement.”\n\n“That too.”\n\n\nAfter breakfast, we returned to the dungeon for our coats.\n\nThe storeroom door was closed.\n\nI looked at it.\n\nSnape caught me.\n\n“No.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Whatever expression you are making.”\n\n“I am looking at a door.”\n\n“You are looking at it sentimentally.”\n\n“I didn't know that was possible.”\n\n“With you, apparently anything is.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“Are you regretting it?”\n\nThe question removed the humor.\n\nSnape went still.\n\n“No.”\n\nImmediate.\n\nCertain.\n\nRelief warmed me.\n\n“Are you?” he asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis shoulders lowered by a fraction.\n\n“Good.”\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“We don't have to decide what it means today.”\n\nHe studied me.\n\n“You enjoy postponing definitions.”\n\n“I enjoy not turning one kiss into a legal proceeding before the actual legal proceeding.”\n\n“That is almost sensible.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“I said almost.”\n\nHis hand found mine.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“We can do this,” I said.\n\n“The hearing?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked down at our hands.\n\n“And this?”\n\nMy heart stumbled.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nSnape's thumb moved over my knuckles.\n\n“You cannot know that.”\n\n“No. Neither can you.”\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“But I want to try.”\n\nA long pause.\n\n“So do I,” he said.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nThe Ministry atrium was worse than I expected.\n\nReporters crowded behind a barrier. Cameras flashed. Quills wrote without hands. Snape went rigid the instant we stepped from the Floo.\n\nHis hand left mine.\n\nNot rejection.\n\nProtection.\n\nThe gesture still hurt.\n\nHe saw it.\n\n“Not here,” he murmured.\n\nI nodded.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHis eyes searched my face.\n\n“You understand?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe public did not get this yet.\n\nMaybe ever.\n\nIt was ours before it became anyone else's story.\n\nA reporter shouted, “Professor Snape, do you expect acquittal?”\n\nAnother: “Were you romantically obsessed with Lily Potter?”\n\nSnape stopped.\n\nI felt the temperature drop around him.\n\nThe second reporter raised a Quick-Quotes Quill.\n\n“Is it true your Patronus remained hers because you could never love anyone else?”\n\nMy stomach twisted.\n\nNot jealousy.\n\nFury.\n\nThey had turned a dead woman and a living man's grief into entertainment.\n\nSnape's face became blank.\n\nHe began walking again.\n\nI followed.\n\nThe reporter called after us, “Do you think Lily Potter would forgive you?”\n\nSnape faltered.\n\nOne step.\n\nBarely.\n\nI moved beside him.\n\nNot touching.\n\nJust there.\n\nHe kept walking.\n\nInside the lift, the doors closed.\n\nThe silence was immediate.\n\nSnape stared at the brass numbers above us.\n\n“I hate them,” I said.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“How refreshingly nuanced.”\n\n“I contain multitudes.”\n\n“Apparently.”\n\nThe lift descended.\n\n“I wanted to hex him,” I admitted.\n\n“Which one?”\n\n“The one who asked about Lily.”\n\n“That narrows nothing.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nThen Snape said, “Thank you for not.”\n\n“For your sake or mine?”\n\n“Both.”\n\nThe hearing chamber waited below.\n\nBefore the doors opened, Snape turned toward me.\n\nHis face was pale.\n\n“After,” he said.\n\nI understood.\n\nAfter the hearing.\n\nAfter the testimony.\n\nAfter whatever judgment came.\n\nWe would return to this.\n\nTo us.\n\nI held his gaze.\n\n“After,” I agreed.\n\nThe doors opened.\n\nWe stepped out separately.\n\nIt did not feel like distance.\n\nIt felt like a promise.\\n\\nThe lift stopped two floors below the hearing chamber.\\n\\nA witch from the Department of Magical Law Enforcement stepped inside carrying three folders and the exhausted expression of someone who had spent the morning being shouted at by people with inherited seats. She recognized Snape immediately.\\n\\nHer eyes widened.\\n\\nThen, to her credit, she looked at the floor numbers instead of staring.\\n\\nSnape became so still beside me that I could feel the effort of it.\\n\\nI did not reach for his hand. Not because I did not want to. Because he had chosen distance in the atrium, and I wanted that choice to remain his instead of becoming something he had to defend.\\n\\nThe witch got off one floor later.\\n\\nThe doors closed.\\n\\nSnape exhaled.\\n\\n“You are thinking very loudly,” he said.\\n\\n“I thought that was your specialty.”\\n\\n“Yours is more obvious.”\\n\\nI looked at him. “Do you want me farther away?”\\n\\nHis head turned sharply.\\n\\n“No.”\\n\\n“Closer?”\\n\\nHe glanced at the brass doors, as though the Ministry itself might file a report on the answer.\\n\\n“Not here.”\\n\\n“Okay.”\\n\\nThat was all.\\n\\nNo punishment. No sulking. No test hidden inside the question.\\n\\nThe lift continued upward.\\n\\nSnape stared at the numbers changing over the door. “You make boundaries sound offensively simple.”\\n\\n“They are simple.”\\n\\n“They are not easy.”\\n\\n“No.” I shifted the file under my arm. “Those are different things.”\\n\\nHe considered that.\\n\\nThe doors opened onto a quieter corridor. A clerk waited beside a carved oak door with a clipboard. Beyond the windows, London was gray with rain.\\n\\n“Mr. Snape,” she said. “They are nearly ready.”\\n\\n“Of course they are.”\\n\\n“You may wait here.”\\n\\nShe indicated a bench.\\n\\nSnape looked at it as though the Ministry had provided a personal insult with cushions.\\n\\nI sat.\\n\\nAfter a moment, he sat beside me.\\n\\nNot touching.\\n\\nClose enough that the sleeve of his robe brushed mine when either of us breathed too deeply.\\n\\nWe waited.\\n\\nA clock ticked at the far end of the corridor. Papers rustled behind the hearing-room door. Somewhere nearby, a man argued in a whisper about procedural authority.\\n\\nSnape's fingers tapped once against his knee. Stopped. Began again.\\n\\nI opened the Ministry file and pretended to read.\\n\\n“Page twelve,” he said.\\n\\n“What?”\\n\\n“If you intend to pretend you are occupied, page twelve contains enough bureaucratic nonsense to justify your expression.”\\n\\nI turned to page twelve.\\n\\nHe was right.\\n\\n“This sentence has four semicolons.”\\n\\n“Criminal.”\\n\\n“Should we report it?”\\n\\n“After the hearing.”\\n\\nThe words landed between us.\\n\\nAfter.\\n\\nNot if.\\n\\nI closed the file.\\n\\nThe clerk opened the door. “Mr. Snape?”\\n\\nHe stood.\\n\\nFor one second his hand hovered near mine.\\n\\nThen his fingers touched the back of my wrist—briefly, deliberately, hidden by the fall of our sleeves.\\n\\nIt was not a kiss.\\n\\nIt was not a declaration.\\n\\nIt was enough to tell me he remembered the promise.\\n\\nAfter.\\n\\nI followed him into the chamber.","word_count":1601},"after-always-29":{"number":29,"title":"The Hearing","content":"The Wizengamot chamber was built to make one person feel small.\n\nRows of benches rose in a dark semicircle. Wizards and witches in plum-colored robes looked down toward a single chair in the center. Torches burned blue-white along stone walls. Every whisper multiplied under the high ceiling.\n\nSnape sat alone.\n\nI sat behind the witness rail with McGonagall, Professor Flitwick, Madam Pomfrey, and three people from the Ministry evidence office.\n\nHarry Potter sat two rows ahead.\n\nHe looked older than the photographs from the war and younger than the burden attached to his name.\n\nWhen Snape entered, Harry stood.\n\nSnape noticed.\n\nHis face gave nothing away.\n\nThe hearing began.\n\nKingsley Shacklebolt presided, not as sole judge but as Minister and chair of the review panel. His voice was calm.\n\n“Severus Snape, this proceeding concerns your legal status following the fall of Tom Riddle, your acknowledged membership in the Death Eaters, your subsequent service as an informant and operative, your conduct as Headmaster of Hogwarts, and complaints regarding your conduct as a teacher.”\n\nSnape's mouth tightened at the final clause.\n\nGood, I thought.\n\nAll of it.\n\nThe first questions were factual.\n\nWhen did he join?\n\nShortly after leaving Hogwarts.\n\nDid he accept the Dark Mark voluntarily?\n\nYes.\n\nDid he pass information to Voldemort concerning a prophecy?\n\nYes.\n\nDid he understand whom it would endanger?\n\nNot initially.\n\nWhen he discovered Lily Potter was a target, did he ask Voldemort to spare her?\n\nYes.\n\nDid he ask for James Potter or Harry Potter to be spared at that time?\n\nNo.\n\nThe chamber shifted.\n\nSnape did not.\n\nHis answers were clipped, exact, merciless toward himself.\n\nThen came the turn.\n\nDid he approach Dumbledore?\n\nYes.\n\nDid he thereafter provide intelligence against Voldemort?\n\nYes.\n\nFor how long?\n\nYears.\n\nDid he protect Harry Potter?\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHarry looked down.\n\nThe evidence followed.\n\nMission logs.\n\nLetters.\n\nDumbledore's stored instructions.\n\nOur potion analyses showing deliberate sabotage of coercive draughts.\n\nThe hidden Carrow cache.\n\nDetention rosters altered during Snape's year as Headmaster.\n\nStatements from students.\n\nSome grateful.\n\nSome furious.\n\nOne described how Snape had redirected a punishment that might have left permanent injury.\n\nAnother described being humiliated in Potions until they abandoned a planned career.\n\nSnape listened to both with the same still face.\n\nWhen asked about the second, he said, “The complaint is accurate.”\n\nA panel member blinked.\n\n“You do not contest it?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Do you believe your conduct was justified by maintaining discipline?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“House rivalry?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then why did you behave that way?”\n\nSnape's jaw tightened.\n\n“Because I was angry, biased, and cruel.”\n\nThe chamber went very quiet.\n\nI watched his hands.\n\nSteady.\n\nOnly the right thumb moved against his palm.\n\nA self-soothing motion so small no reporter would ever notice.\n\nI did.\n\nHarry testified after lunch.\n\nHe walked to the witness chair and looked directly at Snape.\n\nThe room seemed to hold its breath.\n\nHarry did not call him a hero.\n\nFor that alone, I could have hugged him.\n\nHe described facts.\n\nCounter-jinxing a broom during his first year.\n\nTeaching him Expelliarmus indirectly through the dueling club.\n\nOcclumency lessons that were damaging as well as necessary.\n\nProtection.\n\nHostility.\n\nDumbledore's plan.\n\nThe doe Patronus.\n\nThe sword of Gryffindor.\n\nSnape's memories at the end.\n\nThen Harry said, “He hated my father. Sometimes he punished me for it. That was wrong. He also saved my life more than once. That is true too.”\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\nBoth.\n\nSnape's eyes closed for half a second.\n\nHarry continued.\n\n“I don't think what he did for us means the things he did to students don't matter. And I don't think the things he did wrong mean we get to pretend he didn't help defeat Voldemort.”\n\nKingsley watched him carefully.\n\n“What are you asking this panel to do, Mr. Potter?”\n\n“Tell the truth.”\n\nSnape looked at him then.\n\nSomething passed between them.\n\nToo private for me to interpret.\n\nGood.\n\nThe last witness called was me.\n\nI had not expected it.\n\nMy stomach dropped when my name was spoken.\n\nSnape's head snapped toward the rail.\n\nI stood.\n\nThe walk to the chair felt longer than any corridor at Hogwarts.\n\nI sat.\n\nThe panel asked about the audit.\n\nI described the six sabotaged phials.\n\nThe hidden Carrow cache.\n\nThe altered records.\n\nSnape's attempt to burn the list of names.\n\nHis face tightened.\n\nI told that truth too.\n\n“And what prevented destruction of the records?” a witch asked.\n\n“I did.”\n\n“Did Mr. Snape cooperate after that?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Voluntarily?”\n\n“After an argument.”\n\nA few people smiled.\n\nSnape did not.\n\n“What is your professional assessment of him?”\n\nDangerous question.\n\nI looked at the panel.\n\n“As a potioneer?”\n\n“As a person you have worked closely with.”\n\nThe room seemed to narrow.\n\nSnape looked at me.\n\nI remembered every version I refused.\n\nMonster.\n\nMartyr.\n\nBroken man.\n\nSecret saint.\n\n“I don't think that's a professional question,” I said.\n\nMurmurs.\n\nThe witch frowned. “You may still answer.”\n\n“No. I can tell you what I observed. He is an exceptional potioneer. He is exacting. He is difficult. He attempted to protect people whose names appeared in compromised records. He accepted corrections to those records when challenged. He has acknowledged harmful conduct toward students without asking me to excuse it. He has repeatedly provided evidence against his own interest when accuracy required it.”\n\nI paused.\n\n“He is not simple enough for a character assessment to be useful evidence.”\n\nSnape's face changed.\n\nJust slightly.\n\nThe witch leaned back.\n\n“Do you trust him?”\n\nThat question was personal.\n\nI could have objected.\n\nI looked at Snape.\n\n“Yes,” I said.\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n“With what?” she asked.\n\n“Laboratory safety. Evidence. My boundaries. Information that could damage him.”\n\nAnother pause.\n\n“My no.”\n\nThe chamber went quiet.\n\nSnape looked down.\n\nThe questioning ended soon after.\n\nA clerk approached the witness rail with a correction slip. One of the earlier reports had copied a date from the year before the war instead of the year itself. It changed which school term the complaint referred to.\n\n“Can we amend it?” she asked Kingsley.\n\nHe examined the entry, then directed her to read the correction aloud. He did not let a witness silently overwrite the old copy. The original would remain with a dated note beside it.\n\nSnape sat utterly still. He had spent years asking children to be exact in their work. Now exactness was being applied to his own record, and it did not flatter him.\n\nHarry leaned forward. “That was the term I remember.”\n\nThe clerk looked toward Severus.\n\n“Do you contest the correction?” she asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nHe might have offered a justification. He might have explained that the class had been frightened, that the school itself was dangerous, that no one understood the pressure he had been under. Instead he looked down at the sheet in front of him.\n\n“The date is correct,” he said.\n\nA woman in the upper row began whispering that it was absurd to put the savior of Hogwarts on trial for schoolroom cruelty. Another witness asked to have her own complaint included in the public summary. Nobody could turn the hearing into a single verdict on his entire life.\n\nMy palms were wet. I pressed them against my skirt and thought about how much easier it would have been if love allowed me to pick which evidence counted. It didn't.\n\nWhen the clerk returned to her desk, Severus looked back at me once. I didn't smile to reassure him that everything would be fine. I held his gaze until he turned forward.\n\nI could want him to have a future without demanding that the people he had hurt make that future comfortable.\n\n\nThe panel withdrew.\n\nWe waited for nearly two hours.\n\nNo one spoke much.\n\nHarry sat on one side of the corridor.\n\nSnape sat on the other.\n\nAt one point Harry stood, crossed the space, and offered Snape a cup of water.\n\nSnape took it.\n\n“Thank you,” he said.\n\nHarry nodded.\n\nThat was all.\n\nIt felt like enough.\n\nWhen the doors reopened, everyone returned.\n\nKingsley read the decision.\n\nSnape would not be prosecuted for actions taken while acting as Dumbledore's operative or for Dumbledore's death, which the evidence established had been carried out under prior agreement.\n\nHis earlier Death Eater service was formally acknowledged in the record.\n\nHis later cooperation and wartime service were also formally acknowledged.\n\nNo prison sentence.\n\nNo loss of wand rights.\n\nA civil review of former-student complaints would remain available.\n\nHis professional teaching status would be left to Hogwarts and the Board of Governors.\n\nNo declaration of heroism.\n\nNo erasure.\n\nLegal status.\n\nPublic record.\n\nConsequences.\n\nExactly what I had told him the Ministry could provide.\n\nSnape stood.\n\nFor a moment he looked as though he had no idea what to do with being allowed to leave.\n\nThen Kingsley said, “Mr. Snape.”\n\nHe looked back.\n\n“Thank you for your testimony.”\n\nSnape's mouth tightened.\n\n“I would prefer never to repeat it.”\n\nKingsley almost smiled.\n\n“A reasonable preference.”\n\nOutside the chamber, reporters waited upstairs.\n\nSnape looked at me.\n\n“After,” he said.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“After.”\n\nThis time, before the lift doors opened, his hand found mine.","word_count":1515},"after-always-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Laboratory at Spinner's End","content":"The first wall came down on a Saturday.\n\nNot the house wall. Severus would have murdered me, survived Azkaban, and argued the conviction on grammatical grounds if I had suggested knocking through his sitting room without a structural survey.\n\nThe wall belonged to the old coal shed behind Spinner's End.\n\nIt had been leaning for years with the exhausted determination of something too stubborn to collapse while Severus was watching. We had decided the shed could become a proper laboratory annex: separate ventilation, reinforced floor, warded storage, running water, emergency exit, and enough distance from the kitchen that an exploding cauldron would not season dinner.\n\nI had expected Severus to hire builders.\n\nHe hired builders.\n\nThen he stood over them like an avenging gargoyle until the foreman finally put down his measuring tape and said, “Professor, either you let us do the work or you do it yourself.”\n\nSeverus considered this.\n\nI caught his sleeve.\n\n“We are paying them.”\n\n“They are six millimeters off the plan.”\n\n“They haven't built the wall yet.”\n\n“They are contemplating it incorrectly.”\n\nThe foreman stared at me.\n\nI stared back with the exhausted solidarity of someone who had chosen this man voluntarily.\n\n“I'll keep him inside,” I promised.\n\n“You will do no such thing,” Severus said.\n\nFive minutes later he was inside.\n\nHe stood at the kitchen window with his arms crossed while masonry crashed outside.\n\n“I dislike them.”\n\n“They're competent.”\n\n“A separate issue.”\n\nI pushed a mug of coffee toward him. He had slowly, grudgingly admitted that tea was not the only civilized hot beverage in existence. He still behaved as though this concession might be used against him in court.\n\nOn the table between us lay the plans for the laboratory.\n\nThey were his plans, though my notes covered the margins. Fume hood. Isolation cupboard. Two-person ward release for the most dangerous ingredients. Accessible emergency shutoffs from either side of the room. Inventory ledger duplicated magically and on paper. No unmarked storage, no private cursed cache, no experimental substances without a second person knowing they existed.\n\nThe safeguards were not because I distrusted him.\n\nThey were because I trusted what we had learned.\n\nSecrets had saved lives during the war.\n\nSecrets had also nearly killed us after it.\n\n“You moved the restricted cabinet,” he said.\n\n“Three feet.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“So if someone collapses by the main bench, the cabinet door can still open without hitting them.”\n\nHe looked at the plan.\n\nThen at me.\n\nThen back at the plan.\n\n“Annoyingly sensible.”\n\n“Put it on my gravestone.”\n\nHis face changed.\n\nOnly slightly. Anyone else might have missed it.\n\nI did not.\n\n“Bad joke?” I asked.\n\n“Poorly timed.”\n\nI reached across the table and placed my hand palm-up beside his. Not touching. Offering.\n\nAfter a moment, he set his fingers into mine.\n\nThe scar at his throat was paler now, but some mornings his voice still rasped. Some evenings his left hand shook when he was tired. Healing had become less dramatic and more annoying, which Madam Pomfrey had declared an excellent sign.\n\nI traced one knuckle with my thumb.\n\n“I'm not planning on needing the gravestone soon.”\n\n“You have demonstrated a troubling attraction to hazardous professionals.”\n\n“One hazardous professional.”\n\n“Questionable judgment remains questionable judgment even when specific.”\n\nOutside, another section of wall crashed down.\n\nSeverus winced.\n\n“I can hear you mentally correcting their mortar ratio.”\n\n“It is wrong.”\n\n“You are looking through glass from twenty feet away.”\n\n“It is visibly wrong.”\n\nI laughed, and his thumb rubbed once over the side of my hand.\n\nThat gesture still did things to me I refused to dignify with analysis.\n\nOn the third day, the foreman arrived with a second set of plans.\n\n“The drainage isn't where the first drawing says,” he told us. “We opened the ground and found a pipe running straight across your proposed storage wall.”\n\nSeverus took the paper from him and inspected the measurements. “That pipe has no business being there.”\n\n“It's been there forty years,” the foreman said. “Seems fairly committed.”\n\nI bit my cheek. Severus could terrorize a classroom with a single raised eyebrow, but the pipe was proving immune.\n\nHe wanted to redirect it immediately. The foreman told him the neighboring kitchen shared the line; any work needed permission from the woman next door. She was home, watching the proceedings from behind her curtains.\n\n“Did you know about the connection?” I asked Severus.\n\n“No.” The answer sounded personally offensive to him.\n\nWe knocked on the neighbor's door together. She listened, declined the proposed afternoon shutdown, and suggested Tuesday when she would be visiting her sister.\n\nSeverus began to explain the obvious importance of his laboratory. I touched his sleeve.\n\n“She already offered a day,” I whispered.\n\nHe stopped. Then he asked what the disruption would cost her, including the food she would need to move. He added it to the contractor's written estimate instead of assuming a neighbor should subsidize his work with inconvenience.\n\nInside, the kettle clicked as it cooled. I watched him stand over the revised plan, furious at a plumbing diagram and careful not to make the fury somebody else's problem.\n\n“I understand why you never became an architect,” I said.\n\n“An architect would have put the pipe in the correct place.”\n\n“You're describing a miracle, not a profession.”\n\nHe snorted. Later, when the laborers arrived for the planned work, he had tea waiting for them. It was bitter enough to strip paint and served in clean cups. The foreman drank it without comment.\n\n\nThe laboratory took six weeks.\n\nDuring that time, Severus taught his seminar, consulted twice for St. Mungo's, and revised an article on antivenom interactions so aggressively that the editor sent back a note asking whether three pages of criticism were strictly necessary.\n\n“They asked for peer review,” he said when I pointed this out.\n\n“They asked whether the conclusions were supported.”\n\n“They were not.”\n\n“You also corrected their comma placement.”\n\n“Accuracy should not be compartmentalized.”\n\nI kissed his cheek.\n\nHe lost his place in the manuscript.\n\nI considered that a public service.\n\nWhen the laboratory was finally finished, the builders refused to let Severus inspect it until they had packed their tools.\n\nCowards, but intelligent ones.\n\nWe entered together.\n\nThe room smelled of new wood, stone dust, metal, and the faint herbal bite of wards settling into place. Wide counters ran along two walls. Ingredient drawers were labeled in Severus's handwriting because he had rejected mine as “cheerful to the point of negligence.” The central island held two cauldron stations with independent heat controls. A deep sink occupied the far corner beneath a window that looked toward the narrow strip of sky above the neighboring roofs.\n\nIt was not beautiful in the conventional sense.\n\nIt was better.\n\nIt was usable.\n\nIt was safe.\n\nIt was ours without requiring either of us to disappear into the other.\n\nSeverus walked slowly through the room, touching almost nothing.\n\nAt the restricted cabinet he tested the two-person ward. His wand released one side. Mine released the other. The doors opened with a soft click.\n\n“Functional,” he said.\n\n“That is the most romantic thing you've ever said.”\n\n“Your standards are catastrophic.”\n\n“Who am I dating?”\n\nHis look should have stripped varnish.\n\nI grinned.\n\nOn the central table sat one final object covered by a cloth.\n\nSeverus noticed immediately.\n\n“What is that?”\n\n“Something I had made.”\n\nSuspicion sharpened his face. “Why?”\n\n“Because I enjoy danger.”\n\nI pulled away the cloth.\n\nA brass plaque rested underneath.\n\nNot Professor Severus Snape.\n\nNot Headmaster.\n\nNot Half-Blood Prince.\n\nNot war hero, spy, traitor, Death Eater, Order member, murderer, savior, victim, bully, or any of the other names the world had used as if one noun could finish him.\n\nIt read:\n\nSPINNER'S END POTIONS LABORATORY\nS. SNAPE & ASSOCIATE\nRESEARCH — ANTIDOTES — CONSULTATION\n\nHe stared at it for so long I became uncertain.\n\n“I left myself unnamed,” I said. “On purpose. It can stay that way unless I ever want otherwise. I thought—”\n\n“I know what you thought.”\n\nHis voice was rough.\n\nI stopped.\n\nHe lifted the plaque with both hands.\n\n“Associate,” he said.\n\n“You object?”\n\n“Profoundly.”\n\nI blinked.\n\nHe set the plaque down.\n\n“You are not my assistant.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Nor an adjunct to my reputation.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHis eyes met mine. “Partner.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n“In work?”\n\n“In this room,” he said. Then, with the expression of a man voluntarily stepping off a cliff, “And elsewhere, should you continue to insist upon it.”\n\nI laughed softly. “I have been fairly insistent.”\n\n“Pathologically.”\n\nI found a pencil and crossed out ASSOCIATE.\n\nHe watched me write PARTNER underneath.\n\nIt was crooked.\n\nHe took the pencil from me and rewrote it neatly.\n\n“Control problem,” I said.\n\n“Penmanship problem.”\n\n“We can have both.”\n\nHe put the pencil down.\n\nThen he kissed me in the middle of the empty laboratory.\n\nThere was no urgency in it. No near-death terror. No argument to propel us across a line we had been circling. Just his hand at the back of my neck, my fingers curled lightly into the front of his shirt, and the strange ordinary miracle of being able to take our time.\n\nWhen we separated, I rested my forehead against his.\n\n“Functional?” I whispered.\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“Barely.”\n\nOutside, one of the builders shouted that they had forgotten a bucket.\n\nSeverus opened his eyes with murder in them.\n\nI laughed so hard I had to hold onto him.\n\nHe complained all the way to the door.\n\nHe did not let go of my hand.","word_count":1585},"after-always-38":{"number":38,"title":"The Shape of Light","content":"The first time my Patronus held its shape, Severus insulted it.\n\nI had expected many things from the moment. Wonder, perhaps. Relief. A rare expression of approval. Some quiet acknowledgment that months of failed silver mist had finally become something more substantial.\n\nInstead he watched the bright form race once around the abandoned classroom, looked down his nose at me, and said, “Your casting arm is still too tense.”\n\nI stared at him.\n\nThe Patronus dissolved.\n\n“You are unbelievable.”\n\n“Your success does not retroactively improve your technique.”\n\n“I produced a full Patronus.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“A full one.”\n\n“I was present.”\n\n“And that's what you say?”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI pointed at him. “You are enjoying this.”\n\n“Immensely.”\n\nThe second attempt was worse because I was laughing.\n\nSilver fog burst from my wand and spread across the floor like embarrassed weather.\n\nSeverus folded his arms.\n\n“Again.”\n\n“You sabotaged me.”\n\n“If sarcasm can defeat your defensive magic, the Dementors have been approaching the problem with unnecessary complexity.”\n\nI muttered something rude.\n\nHis eyebrows rose. “Again.”\n\nMonths earlier, the first Dementor breach had ended with his doe Patronus blazing through the corridor while I stood half-frozen beside him. At the time, the sight had felt like trespassing on something private. The shape was so tied to the story everyone knew—or thought they knew—that I had not known where to put my eyes.\n\nNow the doe stood quietly at the edge of the room while I reset my grip.\n\nSeverus had cast her to demonstrate stability.\n\nShe was beautiful.\n\nShe was also not Lily Evans.\n\nThat distinction had taken me longer to understand than I liked admitting.\n\nA Patronus could represent love. Memory. Protection. A part of the self shaped by another person. It was not a ghost summoned for comparison.\n\nThe doe did not look at me accusingly.\n\nSeverus did not look at her romantically.\n\nShe simply waited in silver light.\n\n“Your wrist,” he said.\n\nI adjusted it.\n\n“Less force.”\n\n“That's rich coming from you.”\n\n“I am capable of subtlety.”\n\n“You wore black robes dramatically through corridors for twenty years.”\n\n“That was efficient fabric.”\n\n“Your robes billowed indoors.”\n\n“Ventilation.”\n\nI laughed again.\n\nThe doe's ears flicked as though even she had opinions.\n\nSeverus glared at his own Patronus.\n\nI nearly lost the spell before casting it.\n\nThen I found the memory.\n\nNot the first kiss.\n\nNot the hearing.\n\nNot the night he told me he loved me with the same expression he might have used to report a structural defect.\n\nI chose the kitchen at Spinner's End on an ordinary morning.\n\nRain against the glass. Severus reading an article and insulting the methodology under his breath. My mug warming my hands. His foot touching mine beneath the table and staying there without either of us acknowledging it.\n\nA happiness small enough to trust.\n\n“Expecto Patronum.”\n\nSilver erupted from my wand.\n\nThis time the shape formed cleanly.\n\nIt moved through the room in a bright arc, solid enough to cast light over the desks. I felt the spell connect to something inside me—not a person exactly, not one single memory, but the conviction that there were mornings worth surviving toward.\n\nSeverus watched it.\n\nHe did not name its shape.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nThat felt right.\n\nThe reader of my life did not need every private symbol catalogued.\n\nWhen the Patronus faded, Severus was still looking at the place it had been.\n\n“Well?” I asked.\n\nHis gaze shifted to me.\n\n“Your wrist improved.”\n\nI threw a piece of chalk at him.\n\nHe caught it.\n\n“Childish.”\n\n“Deserved.”\n\nHe set the chalk on the desk with maddening precision.\n\nThen he said, “It was excellent.”\n\nThe room went very quiet.\n\nPraise from Severus was never casual. Even after everything between us, he rationed it like a volatile ingredient: carefully, intentionally, and only when he meant every molecule.\n\n“Thank you,” I said.\n\nHe inclined his head.\n\nThe doe still stood beside him.\n\nI looked at her again.\n\n“Does it bother you?” he asked.\n\nThe question was so direct that I needed a moment.\n\n“Your Patronus?”\n\n“You know what I mean.”\n\nI did.\n\nI sat on the edge of a desk. “It used to.”\n\nHis face closed immediately.\n\n“Not because I wanted it to change,” I said. “Because I didn't know what it meant for me if it didn't.”\n\nThe tension remained.\n\nI continued before he could retreat behind it.\n\n“I thought love was supposed to rearrange everything that came before. New relationship, new symbol, new life. Clean slate.”\n\nHis mouth twisted. “A childish model.”\n\n“Convenient model.”\n\n“Those are often synonymous.”\n\nI smiled faintly. “Then I realized I don't actually want you with a clean slate.”\n\nHis eyes sharpened.\n\n“I want you accountable for it,” I said. “I want you changed by what happened. I want you to regret things that should be regretted. I want you to keep what was real without using it as an excuse to stop living.”\n\nThe doe moved closer to him.\n\nSilver light slid over the scar on his throat.\n\n“And her?” he asked.\n\n“Lily?”\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“She mattered.”\n\nThe words were easier now.\n\n“She shaped you. You loved her. You hurt her. You lost her. You built years of your life around trying to protect what she left behind. None of that requires me to be less real.”\n\nHis expression did not change, but his eyes did.\n\n“Nor,” I added, “do I need to become her successor in some position she never held.”\n\nA long silence.\n\nThen he looked toward the doe.\n\n“I spent years believing constancy was the only honorable thing left in me.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“When nearly every other allegiance I had chosen became contaminated by cowardice, anger, vanity, or blood,” he said, “I could at least say that one had not changed.”\n\n“That sounds less like love and more like a sentence.”\n\nHis gaze returned to mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe admission landed softly.\n\nI got off the desk and crossed the room.\n\nHe did not move as I approached.\n\n“Maybe constancy isn't the same as refusing to have a future.”\n\n“No,” he said. “Apparently it is not.”\n\nI held out my hand.\n\nThe doe stepped between us.\n\nFor one absurd second, I wondered whether magical manifestations of the soul could be smug.\n\nSeverus looked down at her.\n\n“Go away.”\n\nShe did not.\n\nI laughed.\n\nHis face turned offended.\n\n“Your Patronus doesn't respect you.”\n\n“It is an extension of my magic.”\n\n“That explains it.”\n\nHe dismissed the spell with a flick of his wand.\n\nThe doe dissolved into a wash of silver that passed over both of us.\n\nWhen the light cleared, his hand was in mine.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\n“My Patronus?”\n\n“Your casting. You are not finished because you succeeded once.”\n\n“Of course I'm not.”\n\n“Competence is repetition.”\n\n“Romance is repetition too, apparently.”\n\n“Do not become sentimental.”\n\n“You built me a labeled shelf in your bathroom.”\n\nHis expression turned dangerous.\n\nI raised my wand before he could answer.\n\nBefore trying again, I asked him to lower his wand.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I'm tired.”\n\nHe stared at me, then at the silver dust still settling across the floor. “You have cast the charm four times.”\n\n“And my shoulder feels like I held up the ceiling for an hour. I want a break.”\n\nHis expression suggested that he had several observations about my technique. He swallowed them with evident difficulty.\n\n“Ten minutes,” he said.\n\n“That sounded suspiciously like an order.”\n\n“A suggestion. Offered with all the warmth I can spare.”\n\nI took the opportunity before he revoked it. He opened a window and let the chill air carry the last of the magical mist into the courtyard. From below came the sound of workers hauling timber to the new laboratory. The ordinary world continued without a patronus to guard it.\n\n“What did you think I'd remember?” I asked.\n\n“For the charm?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I did not presume to choose your memory.”\n\nThat surprised me. He noticed, of course.\n\n“I have had quite enough of other people deciding what ought to make me happy,” he said. “I will not improve the world by inflicting the same arrogance upon you.”\n\nI sat on the desk and stretched my cramped fingers. He offered a glass of water, and I accepted it with both hands.\n\n“I chose a day when I didn't need to prove anything to anyone,” I told him. “You weren't there.”\n\nHis brows lifted.\n\n“Disappointed?”\n\n“Profoundly,” he said. “My vanity is inconsolable.”\n\nI laughed. He stood near enough for his sleeve to brush my knee, but he didn't crowd me. The strange sweetness was not that he had become gentle all at once. He had decided not to turn my success into an extension of his own grief or mine into a lesson about his past.\n\nWhen the break was over, I raised my wand because I wanted to. He checked the room was clear and stepped back.\n\n\nThe next Patronus came more easily.\n\nSo did the one after that.\n\nBy the fifth, Severus stood behind me with one hand lightly correcting the angle of my elbow and his voice close to my ear.\n\nBy the sixth, I was too aware of him to concentrate.\n\nBy the seventh, he accused me of doing it deliberately.\n\nHe was right.\n\nThe Patronus burst into silver anyway.","word_count":1550},"after-always-50":{"number":50,"title":"After the Door Opens","content":"The last chapter of the archive report began with a blank line.\n\nNeri had left it there deliberately, a space where the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger might one day be identified through evidence or voluntary testimony. The committee had finally accepted that not knowing was different from refusing to care.\n\nSeverus stood beside the display case as the first visitors entered.\n\nHe had volunteered an hour of cataloging guidance each week, not because anyone had demanded it, but because he wanted the archive to become a place where students could learn how not to turn uncertainty into fiction.\n\nOrin was the first to arrive.\n\n\"I have a question,\" they said.\n\nSeverus sighed.\n\n\"An auspicious beginning.\"\n\nOrin smiled and opened their notebook.\n\nThey had revised the wartime biography once more, replacing grand claims with citations, conflicting testimony, and a section about people whose accounts remained incomplete. They didn't ask Severus to approve every sentence. They asked whether the evidence had been represented fairly.\n\nHe read the opening.\n\nThen he nodded.\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nOrin looked as though they might faint from happiness.\n\nI took the tea tray to the small table by the window, where Neri had laid out the public maintenance records. The door to the private correspondence room remained closed. The emergency structural records were available through the new sign-out system, and the rules were posted at a height visitors could actually read.\n\nIt wasn't glamorous.\n\nIt was working.\n\n\"Tea?\" I asked Severus.\n\nHe took a cup and sniffed it.\n\n\"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I made tea.\"\n\n\"That much is apparent.\"\n\n\"Is it terrible?\"\n\n\"It is an ambitious assault on the leaves.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe drank it anyway.\n\nOutside, the first light of morning moved across the stone arches. People were gathering for the reopening of the safe canal path, though the lower service passage remained sealed pending more inspection. The square smelled of fresh bread and damp leaves.\n\nIroh passed the window carrying a small kettle. Greg was helping set up chairs. Danse spoke with Toph beside the bridge plan, both of them apparently capable of disagreeing without threatening each other's competence. Maui was carrying a crate of lantern hooks with an expression of remarkable restraint.\n\nSix different lives, overlapping without collapsing into one.\n\nSeverus watched them through the glass.\n\n\"You have a busy day,\" he said.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Are you coming back for dinner?\"\n\n\"Yes. If the inspection runs long, I'll send word before the time we agreed.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThe simplicity of it still mattered.\n\nWe had talked about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory before the first fragile steps of romance. We'd revisited the agreements when schedules changed, when privacy felt like hiding, and when either of us feared being forgotten. There was no final answer that could make those conversations unnecessary forever.\n\nThere was only the decision to keep having them with care.\n\nSeverus set down the cup.\n\n\"Would you like to see the new catalog heading?\" he asked.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nHe showed me a card labeled PRIVATE MATERIAL — CONSENT REQUIRED.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller handwriting, he'd added: HISTORICAL INTEREST DOES NOT CANCEL OWNERSHIP.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"I am capable of useful work.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Your punctuation, however—\"\n\n\"Severus.\"\n\n\"—remains a danger to public safety.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe afternoon passed with visitors asking questions. Some were angry about the blank line. Some wanted a villain's name, a simple answer, or a story that made the valley's past easier to admire. Orin explained why the evidence did not yet support one.\n\nSeverus listened.\n\nHe didn't take over when the apprentice hesitated.\n\nThat might have been the most important lesson he'd learned to teach.\n\nNear the end of the visit, an older man arrived with a weather-stained ledger leaf wrapped in cloth.\n\n\"My mother kept this,\" he told Orin. \"It says a gate was repaired two days before the flood. Does that prove the keeper knew what would happen?\"\n\nOrin's hand hovered over the cloth.\n\nSeverus was listening from the other side of the room. I saw him take a breath and stop himself from answering first.\n\n\"We can look at the date,\" Orin said, \"but not at the paper without your permission. Is this an item you'd like to donate, or are you asking for help understanding it?\"\n\n\"Just help.\"\n\n\"Then you can keep it.\"\n\nThey placed a clean support on the table so the fragile page could rest without being pressed flat. The ink was faded, but the entry remained legible: one hinge replaced, one length of rope purchased, one signature abbreviated.\n\nOrin compared it with a copy of the public repair ledger.\n\n\"This shows maintenance happened,\" they said. \"It doesn't establish what someone knew about the later flood. We would need the weather reports, the instructions issued at the time, and enough context to interpret the abbreviation.\"\n\nThe man looked disappointed.\n\n\"I thought it would be the answer.\"\n\n\"So did I,\" Orin admitted. \"When you brought it in.\"\n\nSeverus stepped forward at last.\n\n\"That is why we are learning to stop before the conclusion,\" he said.\n\nOrin looked relieved but not rescued. They asked the visitor whether a non-identifying transcription could be added to the public evidence list. The man agreed to a copy, declined to donate the original, and asked for a receipt confirming the arrangement.\n\nWe gave him one.\n\nAfter he left, I found Severus standing before the now-empty support.\n\n\"That was difficult for you,\" I said.\n\n\"What was?\"\n\n\"Letting Orin work through the question.\"\n\nHe gave me a dry look.\n\n\"I found seven imprecisions and two potentially catastrophic assumptions.\"\n\n\"How many did you correct?\"\n\n\"One, when invited.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe looked toward the corridor where the apprentice was filing the transcription.\n\n\"They will make mistakes,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And they'll have to repair them.\"\n\n\"Also yes.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"You are distressingly content with imperfect people.\"\n\n\"Only when they let me be one too.\"\n\nFor a moment his expression softened in a way I might once have missed.\n\nHe reached for my hand. I let him take it.\n\nAt closing time, Neri locked the display cases and left the public light on beside the reading shelf. Anyone with a permitted request could return during the next posted hours. A visitor who had been waiting by the archive desk asked whether they could add a name to the Closed Bells record. They had found it in a family notebook, written beside a date and a sketch of the old square.\n\nNeri accepted a copy of the page but did not copy the name into the public ledger.\n\n“Doesn't that solve it?” the visitor asked.\n\n“It gives us another lead,” Neri said. “We have to establish whom the name belonged to, and whether the person consented to being identified.”\n\nSeverus turned toward the display. I saw him prepare the kind of correction that could cut a person's enthusiasm in half. Instead he asked for the ink dates, the notebook's known ownership, and the chain of custody. The questions were dry, irritating and necessary.\n\nThe visitor looked disappointed. “You don't think my grandmother remembered correctly?”\n\n“I think she may have,” Severus said. “I refuse to make your grandmother's memory less trustworthy by claiming it proves more than it does.”\n\nOrin took notes while Neri arranged a private review appointment. No one promised a revelation. They did preserve the clue.\n\nWhen the visitor left, I helped Orin put the copying tools away. One of the drawer labels had fallen off, and the smallest ink bottle had leaked onto the cloth. We cleaned it, wrote a new label and put the damaged fabric aside for proper disposal.\n\n“Quite the dramatic finale,” I said.\n\nSeverus eyed the stain. “If you require fireworks, I can recommend several imprudent former pupils.”\n\nI laughed, then noticed how tired he looked. He had offered an hour and stayed forty minutes longer.\n\n“Your next volunteer shift is next week,” I said. “Not tonight.”\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“Then come home.”\n\nHe folded his coat over one arm and asked Orin whether any work remained that could not wait. Orin checked the ledger, found none, and sent him away.\n\nThe unknown name stayed unknown. The people who might help identify it had a scheduled path into the archive. It was not a triumphant ending, but it was a fairer one than forcing a story to end on command.\n\n\nThe archive would not be held open by one exhausted person's endless willingness to help.\n\nSeverus collected his coat.\n\n\"Ready?\" he asked.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked through the Moonroot Door into Spinner's End. The kitchen was warm, and the table held two bowls, clean cups, and a loaf of bread that hadn't yet been subjected to either of our cooking.\n\nHe had remembered.\n\nI set down the keys and took off my coat.\n\n\"How did the hour go?\" I asked.\n\n\"Orin was tiresome.\"\n\n\"Did you enjoy it?\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was tender and familiar, no longer a promise that he would become someone else, but a choice to meet me as he was. He held me close for a moment, then let me step back to stir the soup.\n\nWe ate while the rain began again outside.\n\nLater, he read by the fire and I wrote in my green notebook. I didn't write a declaration of forever. I wrote what had happened, what we had chosen, and the small ordinary things I wanted to remember.\n\nThe door to the archive would open again tomorrow.\n\nSo would the door to this house, whenever either of us chose to enter.\n\nI watched Severus turn a page and smile at something only he could see.\n\nThen I returned to my own book, grateful that neither of us needed to be a legend to be loved.","word_count":1649}}};for(const s of library.series){const byId=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=byId[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}})();
